A comparator can look at as many keys as the order needs: compare the first key, and only when it ties, the second. To rank words by how often they appear, count them in a map, then copy the entries into a vector, because a map always stays ordered by key. A vector built from two iterators copies everything between them:

```cpp harness
using Entry = std::pair<std::string, int>;
std::unordered_map<std::string, int> counts = {{"cat", 2}, {"the", 3}, {"dog", 2}};
{{code}}
for (const auto& [word, count] : entries) {
    std::cout << word << ' ' << count << '\n';
}
```

```cpp
std::vector<Entry> entries(counts.begin(), counts.end());
std::sort(entries.begin(), entries.end(), [](const Entry& a, const Entry& b) {
    if (a.second != b.second) {
        return a.second > b.second;   // higher count first
    }
    return a.first < b.first;         // a tie: A to Z
});
```

Here `Entry` is `std::pair<std::string, int>`. An `unordered_map` hands its entries over in no particular order, but since the comparator breaks every tie, the sorted result is the same whatever order they arrived in. Leave a tie unbroken and `std::sort` may put the tied entries either way.

`std::tie` from `<tuple>` writes the same comparison in one line. Swapping `a` and `b` on one side flips that key, so this sorts by count from high to low, then by word from A to Z:

```cpp harness
using Entry = std::pair<std::string, int>;
std::vector<Entry> entries = {{"cat", 2}, {"the", 3}, {"dog", 2}};
std::sort(entries.begin(), entries.end(), [](const Entry& a, const Entry& b) {
    {{code}}
});
for (const auto& [word, count] : entries) {
    std::cout << word << ' ' << count << '\n';
}
```

```cpp
return std::tie(b.second, a.first) < std::tie(a.second, b.first);
```

