An interval is a start and an end. A small struct names the two ints, and a `std::vector<Interval>` keeps every interval inline in one heap buffer. A `std::vector<int>` for each interval would cost one heap allocation per interval. Sort a list of them by start with a comparator on `start`:

```cpp
struct Interval {
    int start;
    int end;
};

std::vector<Interval> meetings = {{9, 10}, {1, 3}, {4, 8}, {2, 6}};
std::sort(meetings.begin(), meetings.end(), [](const Interval& a, const Interval& b) {
    return a.start < b.start;
});
std::cout << meetings[0].start << '-' << meetings[0].end << '\n';   // 1-3
```

Once they're sorted, intervals that overlap sit next to each other, so one pass merges them. Keep a vector of merged intervals. When the next interval starts after the last merged one ends, it begins a new block. Otherwise it overlaps, so stretch the last block's end with `std::max`, since a short interval can sit entirely inside a long one:

```cpp harness
struct Interval {
    int start;
    int end;
};

std::vector<Interval> meetings = {{1, 3}, {2, 6}, {4, 8}, {9, 10}};
std::vector<Interval> merged;
for (const auto& cur : meetings) {
    {{code}}
}
for (const auto& m : merged) {
    std::cout << m.start << '-' << m.end << ' ';
}
std::cout << '\n';
```

```cpp
if (merged.empty() || merged.back().end < cur.start) {
    merged.push_back(cur);
} else {
    merged.back().end = std::max(merged.back().end, cur.end);
}
```

`merged.back()` is a reference to the last block, so assigning to `merged.back().end` changes it in place. Problems that pick intervals greedily sort by the end instead, `a.end < b.end`, so the interval that ends first comes first.

example.code:
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

struct Interval {
    int start;
    int end;
};

int main() {
    int n = 0;
    std::cin >> n;
    std::vector<Interval> intervals;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        int start = 0;
        int end = 0;
        std::cin >> start >> end;
        intervals.push_back({start, end});
    }

    std::sort(intervals.begin(), intervals.end(), [](const Interval& a, const Interval& b) {
        return a.start < b.start;
    });

    std::vector<Interval> merged;
    for (const auto& cur : intervals) {
        if (merged.empty() || merged.back().end < cur.start) {
            merged.push_back(cur);
        } else {
            merged.back().end = std::max(merged.back().end, cur.end);
        }
    }

    for (const auto& m : merged) {
        std::cout << m.start << ' ' << m.end << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}

