struct Interval {
    int start;
    int end;
};

int main() {
    int n = 0;
    std::cin >> n;
    std::vector<Interval> meetings;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        int start = 0;
        int end = 0;
        std::cin >> start >> end;
        meetings.push_back({start, end});
    }

    std::sort(meetings.begin(), meetings.end(), [](const Interval& a, const Interval& b) {
        return a.start < b.start;
    });

    std::vector<Interval> busy;
    for (const auto& cur : meetings) {
        if (busy.empty() || busy.back().end < cur.start) {
            busy.push_back(cur);
        } else {
            busy.back().end = std::max(busy.back().end, cur.end);
        }
    }

    int total = 0;
    for (const auto& block : busy) {
        std::cout << block.start << '-' << block.end << '\n';
        total += block.end - block.start;
    }
    std::cout << "busy " << total << " hours" << '\n';
    return 0;
}

steps.body:
Some orders come from no single key. To arrange numbers into the largest number you can glue together, compare two pieces by the two ways of joining them: `9` goes before `5` because `"95"` beats `"59"`, and `3` before `30` because `"330"` beats `"303"`. Keep the numbers as strings, since glued digits are text. Both joined strings have the same length, so `>` compares them digit by digit:

```cpp
std::vector<std::string> parts = {"3", "30", "34", "5", "9"};
std::sort(parts.begin(), parts.end(), [](const std::string& a, const std::string& b) {
    return a + b > b + a;
});
for (const std::string& p : parts) {
    std::cout << p;
}
std::cout << '\n';   // 9534330
```

`std::sort` needs the comparator to be a *strict weak ordering*:

- Two equal pieces give `false`, which is why you write `>` and never `>=`.
- It never says both that `a` goes before `b` and that `b` goes before `a`.
- It is transitive: when `a` goes before `b` and `b` before `c`, `a` goes before `c`. And when `a` ties with `b` and `b` ties with `c`, `a` ties with `c`.

This comparator keeps all three, though proving the last one for glued strings takes some work. One that breaks a rule is undefined behavior, and `std::sort` can even crash.

Watch one edge case: when every number is 0, the glued result is `000`. After sorting, the first piece is the one that leads the number, so if `parts[0]` is `"0"`, every piece is, and the answer is just `0`.

example.code:
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

std::string largestNumber(std::vector<std::string> parts) {
    std::sort(parts.begin(), parts.end(), [](const std::string& a, const std::string& b) {
        return a + b > b + a;
    });
    if (parts[0] == "0") {
        return "0";
    }
    std::string joined;
    for (const std::string& p : parts) {
        joined += p;
    }
    return joined;
}

int main() {
    int q = 0;
    std::cin >> q;
    for (int t = 0; t < q; ++t) {
        int n = 0;
        std::cin >> n;
        std::vector<std::string> parts(n);
        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            std::cin >> parts[i];
        }
        std::cout << largestNumber(parts) << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}

