steps.body:
How many rooms does a schedule need? Sort the start times and the end times on their own, then walk the starts in order with an index `e` into the ends. A meeting that starts before the earliest end still to come needs a new room. Otherwise some meeting has already ended, so the new one takes that room and `e` moves on to the next end:

```cpp
std::vector<int> starts = {0, 5, 10, 15};
std::vector<int> ends = {10, 15, 20, 30};   // both already sorted
int rooms = 0;
std::size_t e = 0;
for (int s : starts) {
    if (s < ends[e]) {
        ++rooms;
    } else {
        ++e;
    }
}
std::cout << rooms << '\n';   // 2
```

Splitting the meetings apart is fine because the question only asks how many overlap, never which ones. `s < ends[e]` lets a meeting that ends at 10 free its room for one that starts at 10. If the rules say a room is still busy at its end time, write `s <= ends[e]`.

This walk is a *sweep line*: it moves through time once, in order, and only the events change the count. The two sorts cost O(n log n), and the walk is O(n).

example.code:
#include <algorithm>
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    int n = 0;
    std::cin >> n;
    std::vector<int> starts(n);
    std::vector<int> ends(n);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        std::cin >> starts[i] >> ends[i];
    }
    std::sort(starts.begin(), starts.end());
    std::sort(ends.begin(), ends.end());

    int rooms = 0;
    std::size_t e = 0;
    for (int s : starts) {
        if (s < ends[e]) {
            ++rooms;
        } else {
            ++e;
        }
    }
    std::cout << "rooms " << rooms << '\n';
    return 0;
}

